# XSH getentropy()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# The page lets getentropy() fail with ENOSYS when there is no source of
# entropy; the tests then report NOTINUSE. Randomness is judged only by
# gross failures that a real random source has negligible chance of
# producing (a 256-byte draw with sixteen or more zero bytes, two 32-byte
# draws that are equal).

assertion  XSH.getentropy.001
anchor     functions/getentropy.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/getentropy.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2024
says       getentropy() returns 0 for lengths 0, 1, 16, 64, 256 and
           {GETENTROPY_MAX}, and does not touch bytes beyond the length.
note       {GETENTROPY_MAX} is at least 256 (XBD limits.h).
test       t/001-lengths.c

assertion  XSH.getentropy.002
anchor     functions/getentropy.html#ERRORS
anchor     functions/getentropy.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2024
says       A length of {GETENTROPY_MAX} + 1 (and a much larger one) gives -1
           with errno EINVAL.
test       t/002-too-long.c

assertion  XSH.getentropy.003
anchor     functions/getentropy.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       The whole requested length is filled with unpredictable data: in
           a 256-byte draw fewer than sixteen bytes are zero, and two
           consecutive 32-byte draws differ.
test       t/003-random-fill.c
